{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"PHASE 1 COMPLETE — proven root cause and caller-safety gate\n\nRoot cause: Python 3.11 asyncio.wait_for has the documented cancellation/completion race in CPython issue 86296. In src/rebar/opcert_service/app.py:76-85 the worker awaits wait_for(asyncio.to_thread(...)); TestClient shutdown cancels that worker at app.py:55-59. When the offloaded fake completes in the same turn, Python 3.11 tasks.py:477-479 returns the child result instead of re-raising the external CancelledError. The worker therefore updates the record, executes queue.task_done(), and loops back to the empty queue with cancelling()==1. Lifespan issued only one cancel and awaits that still-live task without a bound, so Starlette never receives lifespan.shutdown.complete and pytest-timeout kills the worker. Python 3.12 rewrote wait_for on asyncio.timeout and does not consume this cancellation.\n\nRuntime proof:\n- Deterministic no-thread/no-TestClient harness, identical real _worker: Python 3.11 after release+cancel => done=False, cancelled=False, cancelling=1, record_status=completed, stack=_worker at the next queue read. Python 3.12 => done=True, cancelled=True, record_status=running. This rules out xdist, TestClient, AnyIO, executor shutdown, and shared app state.\n- Tagged real-path trace on the original test under Python 3.11 observed lifespan cancel (task pending), then wait_for returned with current_task().cancelling()==1, then the worker looped; lifespan never joined and the 3s watchdog fired. Python 3.12 joined promptly. All instrumentation was removed; worktree is clean.\n- Intervention probe replaced only Python 3.11 wait_for with Python 3.12 asyncio.timeout semantics: the original target passed 20/20; unpatched baseline failed 3/3.\n- Executor alternative rejected for this incident: a deliberately blocked run_job propagated worker CancelledError and joined lifespan before portal teardown; its executor thread was visibly blocked. The actual failure had an idle executor thread and was still waiting for lifespan shutdown.\n\nThe account explains the CI timing: three Ubuntu/Python 3.11 runs reached the 300s per-test timeout then reported node down on test_matching_guard_is_accepted, while Python 3.12/3.13 passed. The local target reproduces without xdist; xdist only reports the killed worker.\n\nDefect legitimacy: app.py:1-7 promises ACK-fast bounded job processing, app.py:62-66 says no job can wedge the queue, and the existing matching-guard TestClient contract at tests/unit/test_opcert_service_app.py:44-50,137-144 must teardown. This is a defect, not intended behavior.\n\nCaller/dependency sweep: _worker has one caller, opcert lifespan. The app is hosted by uvicorn through infra/compose/Dockerfile.opcert and exercised by test_opcert_service_app.py; no caller behaviorally depends on a consumed cancellation or unbounded shutdown. Expected post-fix behavior: a shutdown cancellation always terminates the worker promptly even when job completion collides with it; accepted-job semantics, per-job timeout behavior, status schemas, and Python 3.12+ behavior remain unchanged."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786248407658467001,"uuid":"a26c4a26-49a5-4a55-828b-05ca040e046a"}